#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114884 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b54258-61d2-549c-94e4-a251f6d2a656" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Differentiation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce098ff9-2ef1-5483-b057-b3fef12af16d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2477" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $\\mathbf f(t) + 2\\mathbf g(t)$ with respect to $t,$ where $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =(4{t}^{2}+9)\\,i-(\\frac{1}{t-2})\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =(4t-1)\\,i+{e}^{3t}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f(t) + 2\\mathbf g(t))' = \\mathbf f'(t) + 2\\mathbf g'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivative of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}+9)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{1}{t-2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,k \\\\  & =8t\\,i+\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k, \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t})j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)k \\\\  & =4\\,i+3{e}^{3t}\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f(t)+2g(t))^{′} & ={f}^{′}(t)+2{g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(8t\\,i+\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k)+2(4\\,i+3{e}^{3t}\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k) \\\\  & =8(t+1)\\,i+(\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}+6{e}^{3t})\\,j-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114885 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19f5f01-948e-5f44-8016-63073ed31dbc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Scalar Function Multiple Rule for Differentiation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3668609c-7f98-53ef-8aa7-6f9c3209dfee" :question/math-academy-id "e-2478" :question/problem "Find $\\big( u(t) \\mathbf g(t) \\big)'$ where $[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ -t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]$ and $u(t) = t.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\big( u(t) \\mathbf g(t) \\big)' = u'(t) \\mathbf{g}(t) + u(t) \\mathbf{g}'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $\\mathbf g'(t)$ and $u'(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)g(t))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(t)g(t)+u(t){g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ -t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+t\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ -t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -t \\\\ 2t{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ -2t \\\\ (2t+1){e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114886 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a146d5d3-2562-5b8b-886e-4cf6519d3282" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Chain Rule for Differentiation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbb15346-028c-59c0-9b20-cd2f408c3bcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-2479" :question/problem "Find $(\\mathbf f \\circ u)'(t),$ if $\\mathbf f(u) = \\ln u \\, \\mathbf i + (u^2+5) \\, \\mathbf j + 4u \\, \\mathbf k$ and $u(t) =2t^3.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f \\circ u)'(t) = \\mathbf{f}'(u(t)) u'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivative of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡u)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({u}^{2}+5)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(4u)k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\,i+2u\\,j+4\\,k, \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})=6{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & ={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{u(t)}\\,i+2u(t)\\,j+4\\,k){u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{t}^{3}}\\,i+2(2{t}^{3})\\,j+4\\,k)\\cdot 6{t}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}\\,i+24{t}^{5}\\,j+24{t}^{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114887 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54a6dbe-42cb-5614-ae0b-611de1719b45" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Product Rules for Differentiation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "228ae66f-9dad-5e6d-b405-9fbe62ecb7e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2480" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf f \\cdot \\mathbf g)(t),$ given the following functions $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\sqrt{t}\\,i+{t}^{2}j \\\\ g(t) & =\\frac{1}{t}i+{e}^{t}j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Using the dot product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{f} \\cdot \\mathbf{g})(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{f}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{g}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{g}(t))\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}f(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+2t\\,j, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}g(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf f \\cdot \\mathbf g)(t)\n&= \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{f}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{g}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{g}(t)) \\[3pt]\n&= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\,\\mathbf i +2t \\, \\mathbf j \\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{1}{t} \\,  \\mathbf i + e^t \\,  \\mathbf j \\right) +\n\\left(\\sqrt{t} \\,  \\mathbf i + t^2 \\,  \\mathbf j \\right) \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{t^2}  \\, \\mathbf i + e^t \\, \\mathbf j  \\right) \\[3pt]\t\t \n&=\\dfrac{1}{2t\\sqrt{t}}  +2te^t    +  \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}}{t^2}\\right)    + t^2e^t \\[3pt]\n&=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}}{2t^2}  -  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}}{t^2} +2te^t   + t^2e^t  \\[3pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}}{2t^2} +\\left(2t+t^2\\right)e^t.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b54258-61d2-549c-94e4-a251f6d2a656" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6272e646-6faf-5cbf-9aa8-bfa1c9a1a5a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,\\,-2t,\\,2t-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305617} {:answer/id #uuid "61492250-b27b-5c47-b317-b118914c4bd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305618} {:answer/id #uuid "41c31433-12c8-5113-bb33-8079400d618f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,\\,2t+1,\\,3{t}^{2}+2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305619} {:answer/id #uuid "12a7c4c0-521d-5b22-8f03-585838f052b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,2t-2,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305620} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc264b1-b837-534c-ae0b-bb800191e149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,2t-2,\\,3{t}^{2}+2t-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fc264b1-b837-534c-ae0b-bb800191e149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,2t-2,\\,3{t}^{2}+2t-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305621} :answer-field/id #uuid "41aec396-46ce-51ab-bf41-4cb56972babe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e49dfe3-04a3-591e-9ea0-48737f115840" :question/math-academy-id "q-127998" :question/problem "Differentiate $f(t)+g(t)$ with respect to $t$, given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨1-2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,(t-1)^{2}⟩, \\\\ g(t) & =⟨t+1,\\,1-2t,\\,{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(f(t)+g(t))^{′}={f}^{′}(t)+{g}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-1)^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,2(t-1)⟩ \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f(t)+g(t))^{′} & ={f}^{′}(t)+{g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,2t-2⟩+⟨1,\\,-2,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,2t-2,\\,3{t}^{2}+2t-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a146d5d3-2562-5b8b-886e-4cf6519d3282" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Chain Rule for Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1688504-491d-5ed1-977d-71a2be217a97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,-\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305645} {:answer/id #uuid "83e2f4cf-588a-592d-9565-6a6c5ef07e7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1+{t}^{2}},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305646} {:answer/id #uuid "eb1bba33-58b4-5a00-b06a-ff91a11fd24a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-\\frac{1}{t+{t}^{2}},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305647} {:answer/id #uuid "4e255fc7-6272-5f92-82e3-d5da680cb14a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{t},-\\frac{1}{t+{t}^{2}},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305648} {:answer/id #uuid "c3894f94-e78d-54bc-9274-7c41774f70f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-\\frac{t}{1+{t}^{2}},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb1bba33-58b4-5a00-b06a-ff91a11fd24a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-\\frac{1}{t+{t}^{2}},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305647} :answer-field/id #uuid "00777e8c-8742-506c-8fd2-c313410fdbd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00ec801b-e22a-5c19-899f-45b6911ab4a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-128570" :question/problem "If $g(u)=⟨\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u+1),\\,1⟩$ and $u(t)=\\frac{1}{t}$, then $(g∘u)^{′}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(g∘u)^{′}(t)=g′(u(t))u′(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(u) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\frac{1}{u}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{u+1},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g∘u)^{′}(t) & =g′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{(u(t))^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{u(t)+1},\\,0⟩\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{t}{1+t},\\,0⟩\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & =⟨1,-\\frac{1}{t+{t}^{2}},0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b54258-61d2-549c-94e4-a251f6d2a656" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cebd29e4-87cb-572b-af0f-e2f507cdff45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2t,0,2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305673} {:answer/id #uuid "86b9e636-95ff-5f96-96da-479f305b5e98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2t-1,0,2t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305674} {:answer/id #uuid "95fe6746-9e7e-5186-8569-90337a4cf20b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305675} {:answer/id #uuid "33425daf-de81-52c8-a41e-ea1040b0a9d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨t-1,0,t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305676} {:answer/id #uuid "aa876634-6b62-550b-b0a0-cf726acb311d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2t+1,2t+1,2t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86b9e636-95ff-5f96-96da-479f305b5e98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2t-1,0,2t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305674} :answer-field/id #uuid "23a8d32d-d998-5d9c-8f0b-312896fbb02c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8795bddd-70ad-516e-a9a3-68891cf5e70d" :question/math-academy-id "q-32503" :question/problem "Differentiate $f(t)+g(t)+h(t)$ with respect to $t$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨-t,\\,0,t⟩ \\\\ h(t) & =⟨-{t}^{2},0,{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f(t)+g(t)+h(t))^{′}={f}^{′}(t)+{g}^{′}(t)+{h}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\ {h}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f(t)+g(t)+h(t))^{′} & ={f}^{′}(t)+{g}^{′}(t)+{h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨0,0,0⟩+⟨-1,0,1⟩+⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t-1,\\,0,\\,2t+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19f5f01-948e-5f44-8016-63073ed31dbc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Scalar Function Multiple Rule for Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "611ca792-d600-5e6f-99a1-3a89dd0cde64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10t-8 \\\\ 12t-4 \\\\ 6t-5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305722} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba29446-9aa3-5adf-a382-bb1b9ff76751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12t-15 \\\\ 14t-7 \\\\ 18t-2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305723} {:answer/id #uuid "14784451-5ea7-55a7-b322-97172ecb01e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8t-14 \\\\ 6t-5 \\\\ 10t-3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305724} {:answer/id #uuid "a372e0b5-6844-5cdf-a406-2261db425471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4t-7 \\\\ 3t-2 \\\\ 5t-1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305725} {:answer/id #uuid "b531760c-4b94-52f4-8341-0dbfbb12cd4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16t-14 \\\\ 12t-5 \\\\ 20t-3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b531760c-4b94-52f4-8341-0dbfbb12cd4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16t-14 \\\\ 12t-5 \\\\ 20t-3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305726} :answer-field/id #uuid "53a4f43c-f2c7-554a-9b2f-7a24129f32d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de33fa52-4e1e-55d5-aa30-80045075815d" :question/math-academy-id "q-71225" :question/problem "Find ${(u(t)f(t))}^{′}$ where $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4t-1 \\\\ 3t+2 \\\\ 5t+6\\end{aligned}]$ and $u(t)=2t-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n{(u(t)f(t))}^{′}={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t+6)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-3)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(t)f(t))}^{′} & ={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}4t-1 \\\\ 3t+2 \\\\ 5t+6\\end{aligned}]+(2t-3)[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8t-2 \\\\ 6t+4 \\\\ 10t+12\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8t-12 \\\\ 6t-9 \\\\ 10t-15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16t-14 \\\\ 12t-5 \\\\ 20t-3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
